A||N||6||FieroJoe||Injector O-rings question!||||1||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||12-27-2001||12:48 AM||||I am going to replace the o-rings on all of my injectors since they are out and look worn as well.  Are there special o-rings for these applications, or can I just use the o-rings I sell at the hardware store I work at?  The rings I sell are general purpose, and I'm not sure if they are resistant to fuel or not.  I typically sell them to customers rebuilding faucet valves, but there have been other instances where they are used in other applications...<P>So, would these general purpose ones work as long as I find ones that fit right?  Will the same type work for in the fuel line fittings (those have come up missing)?<P>Secondly, will it hurt if I use a little silicone grease, like a real light coating on the o rings, while installing them to keep them from binding?  I can't see why not but I would just like to make sure.<P>Thanks!<BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierojoesig2.gif">  <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000001||00lE||12-27-2001||01:17 AM||||Most fuel injector o-rings are made from Viton.  Although I am sure that a normal rubber o-ring from a hardware store may work, it is probably not worth the chance condsidering how inexpensive the correct replacement o-rings would be.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||12-27-2001||03:13 AM||||in my haynes manuals, it suggests using very fine oil<P>like those machine household light oil you get at the hardware store.<P>im pretty sure the silicone will clog the injector and or ruin the oxygen sensor.<P>i would go to a car store and get the special o rings.  Im not sure if the ones from the hardware store is compatible with gas.||||reg||1
Z||000003||avengador1||12-27-2001||10:07 AM||||You can lubricate the "o" rings with a little bit of vaseline petroleum jelly. It's fuel soluble and shouldn't hurt the rings, just remember to use very little.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroJoe||12-27-2001||11:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierospeeder:<BR><B>i would go to a car store and get the special o rings.  Im not sure if the ones from the hardware store is compatible with gas.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I asked for them at Autozone, and they pointed out the generic o rings to me.  I will try a NAPA or Carquest later, maybe they'll know a little more about what they are talking about.||||reg||1
Z||000005||merlin||12-27-2001||08:17 PM||||I actually bought fuel injector O-rings from both Standard Ignition and Borg Warner for my V6 and each of them appeared to be thinner than the stock o-rings.  I tried installing them, but the injectors actually wobbled in the fuel-rail so I decided that I didn't want to take the chance of them leaking.  The brand that seems to match most closely to stock is AC-Delco (part# 217-1379).  You can see a picture at <A HREF="http://www.acdelco.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.acdelco.com</A>  and you can purchase them at <A HREF="http://www.carparts.com." TARGET=_blank>http://www.carparts.com.</A>   Sorry, but I can't seem to locate the part number for the fuel-line o-rings, but I believe that I got them at PepBoys.<P>Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-27-2001||10:46 PM||||I bought mine at PepBoys for $0.50 each.  They worked perfectly.  Don't forget the cold start injector.<P>Lube on the o-ring will not get into the injector.  The o-ring is outside of the inner workings of the injector.||||reg||1
